From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ruhan <35257374+RuhanSA079@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: rk808: allow disabling rk817's internal RTC via
 devicetree

Boards with an external RTC (this project's Radxa CM3 IO uses a
HYM8563 on i2c2, see the next patch) end up with two competing
/dev/rtcN devices, since the rk817 PMIC's own internal RTC is always
registered unconditionally.

The "rk808-rtc" cell in rk817s[] has no cell->of_compatible set, so
mfd_add_device()'s generic disabled-child skip (drivers/mfd/mfd-core.c,
matched via of_device_is_compatible()) never runs for it -- there is
no way to opt out of it from devicetree at all today. Radxa's own
downstream vendor kernel (radxa/kernel) supports exactly this via a
plain child node with no "compatible" property:

	&rk817 {
		rtc {
			status = "disabled";
		};
	};

which can't be matched by the generic of_compatible mechanism (it has
nothing to match against). This adds an explicit, narrowly-scoped
check in rk808_probe() for RK817/RK809 that looks up a child node
named "rtc" and, if present and disabled, filters the "rk808-rtc" cell
out of the cells array before it's handed to devm_mfd_add_devices().

This is opt-in and backward compatible: boards that don't add an "rtc"
child node under their rk817/rk809 PMIC node (i.e. every board except
ours right now) see no behavior change at all, since of_get_child_by_name()
simply returns NULL for them and the existing code path is untouched.

Verified by hand against this kernel's actual headers (mfd_cell.name
is `const char *`, devm_kcalloc/of_get_child_by_name/of_device_is_available
signatures all match this call). Could not do a full `make` compile
check here -- the configured build tree is root-owned (built via
build.sh, which requires root) and too large (12G) to cheaply
duplicate into a writable copy for a one-off verification build.

---
 drivers/mfd/rk808.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/rk808.c b/drivers/mfd/rk808.c
--- a/drivers/mfd/rk808.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/rk808.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/reboot.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/syscore_ops.h>
 #include <linux/pinctrl/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/pinctrl/devinfo.h>
@@ -1318,6 +1319,43 @@
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}

+	/*
+	 * The "rk808-rtc" cell above has no cell->of_compatible, so
+	 * mfd_add_device()'s generic disabled-child skip in mfd-core.c never
+	 * runs for it -- it is always instantiated regardless of what's in
+	 * the devicetree. Boards with an external RTC (e.g. a HYM8563) that
+	 * want to disable this PMIC's internal one via a plain
+	 *   &rk817 { rtc { status = "disabled"; }; };
+	 * need an explicit check here instead, since that child node has no
+	 * "compatible" property for the generic mechanism to match against.
+	 */
+	if (rk808->variant == RK817_ID || rk808->variant == RK809_ID) {
+		struct device_node *rtc_np = of_get_child_by_name(np, "rtc");
+
+		if (rtc_np) {
+			bool rtc_disabled = !of_device_is_available(rtc_np);
+
+			of_node_put(rtc_np);
+			if (rtc_disabled) {
+				struct mfd_cell *filtered;
+				int k, j = 0;
+
+				filtered = devm_kcalloc(&client->dev, nr_cells,
+							sizeof(*filtered),
+							GFP_KERNEL);
+				if (!filtered)
+					return -ENOMEM;
+
+				for (k = 0; k < nr_cells; k++)
+					if (strcmp(cells[k].name, "rk808-rtc"))
+						filtered[j++] = cells[k];
+
+				cells = filtered;
+				nr_cells = j;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	rk808->i2c = client;
 	rk808_i2c_client = client;
 	i2c_set_clientdata(client, rk808);
--
2.43.0
